\subsection{The locale}
\label{sec:locale-setting}

One setting chooses the language of Mentipy's own words on both
surfaces: the slide labels, read when the deck compiles, and the web
pages, read when [[mentipy serve]] starts, whose [[--locale]] flag
overrides it for one lecture.  The words themselves are in the
catalogue (\cref{chap:catalogue}); the store only holds the choice,
with every other key.  The default is English, so a deck that never
sets it renders as before.

The key is [[mentipy.locale]] rather than the more obvious
[[mentipy.language]], because \enquote{language} already means
something else in Mentipy: a pin-text question's or a context
snippet's [[language=]] is a Pygments lexer or [[latex]], the language
of the \emph{content}.  A setting called [[language]] next to those
keywords would invite [[language="python"]] in the configuration and
[[language="sv"]] in a helper call.  [[locale]] is the conventional
name for the language of an interface, and it accepts what a system
locale looks like, [[sv_SE.UTF-8]] included.  The default joins the
built-in table here, so [[mentipy config]] and [[mentipy latex]] list
it with the others.

<<constants>>=
LOCALE_CONFIG = "mentipy.locale"
DEFAULT_LOCALE = "en"
DEFAULT_SETTINGS[LOCALE_CONFIG] = DEFAULT_LOCALE
@

\subsection{The browser command}
\label{sec:open-command}

[[mentipy present]] opens the lecturer's admin page by running a
command with the page's URL.  No one command works everywhere, so the
default depends on the platform: [[xdg-open]] on Linux and the other
Unix systems, the freedesktop.org launcher that desktops provide;
[[open]] on macOS, which has no [[xdg-open]]; and [[explorer]] on
Windows, which opens a URL in the default browser.  The setting
[[mentipy.open_command]] replaces the default, for a particular browser
or a window of its own, and an empty value or [[none]] turns the step
off on the machine; [[present --open-with]] replaces both for one
run.

The default is resolved when it is used, by one small function, rather
than computed into a constant when the module is imported.  The table
of defaults therefore holds [[None]] for the key, which still lists it
in [[mentipy config]], and the tests can try every platform by
changing [[sys.platform]].

<<imports>>=
import sys
<<constants>>=
OPEN_COMMAND_CONFIG = "mentipy.open_command"
DEFAULT_SETTINGS[OPEN_COMMAND_CONFIG] = None
<<functions>>=
def default_open_command() -> str:
    """Return the platform's command for opening a URL in a browser."""

    if sys.platform == "darwin":
        return "open"
    if sys.platform == "win32":
        return "explorer"
    return "xdg-open"
@

<<test functions>>=
@pytest.mark.parametrize(
    "platform, command",
    [
        ("linux", "xdg-open"),
        ("freebsd14", "xdg-open"),
        ("darwin", "open"),
        ("win32", "explorer"),
    ],
)
def test_default_open_command_follows_the_platform(
    monkeypatch, platform, command
):
    monkeypatch.setattr(sys, "platform", platform)

    assert default_open_command() == command
