The environment override makes tests cheap and also gives power users a simple
escape hatch when they want a project-local config file.

<<functions>>=
def config_path() -> Path:
    """Return the configuration file used by mentipy."""

    if override := os.environ.get("MENTIPY_CONFIG"):
        return Path(override)

    return typerconf.platformdirs.user_config_path(
        CONFIG_NAMESPACE,
        appauthor=False,
        ensure_exists=False,
    ) / "config.json"
@

The CLI accepts short keys such as [[port]], but the stored JSON keeps them
under the [[mentipy]] namespace so unrelated settings do not collide.

<<functions>>=
def normalize_config_key(key: str) -> str:
    """Return the fully qualified config key for ``key``."""

    if key.startswith(f"{CONFIG_NAMESPACE}."):
        return key
    return f"{CONFIG_NAMESPACE}.{key}"
@

Loading the configuration is intentionally separated from reading individual
settings.  The rest of the package should not know where the file lives or how
[[typerconf]] persists it; those details are fixed here.  We also seed the
object with a fresh empty mapping, because [[typerconf.Config()]] otherwise
reuses a mutable default across calls, which leaks settings from one missing
config file into the next.

<<functions>>=
def load_config(*, writeback: bool = False) -> typerconf.Config:
    """Return the mentipy configuration."""

    config = typerconf.Config({})
    config.read_config(config_path(), writeback=writeback)
    return config
@

Reading a setting first normalises the key and then falls back to the built-in
defaults.  That two-step lookup is what lets callers pass either [[port]] or
[[mentipy.port]] while still preserving explicit defaults for keys whose value
is normally [[None]].  The generic [[typerconf]] command stores scalar
[[--set]] values as strings, so we also coerce the built-in non-string settings
back to the types the rest of the package expects.

<<functions>>=
def _coerce_setting_value(full_key: str, value: Any) -> Any:
    if isinstance(value, str):
        parsed = parse_config_value(value)
    else:
        parsed = value

    if full_key in (PORT_CONFIG, MIN_PREFIX_CONFIG, HEATMAP_BINS_CONFIG):
        if isinstance(parsed, int):
            return parsed
        return value

    if full_key in (SHOW_URL_CONFIG, ALLOW_IMAGES_CONFIG):
        if isinstance(parsed, bool):
            return parsed
        if isinstance(parsed, int):
            return bool(parsed)

    return value
@

<<functions>>=
def get_setting(key: str, default: Any = None) -> Any:
    """Return the configured value for ``key``, or ``default``."""

    full_key = normalize_config_key(key)
    fallback = DEFAULT_SETTINGS.get(full_key, default)

    try:
        return _coerce_setting_value(full_key, load_config().get(full_key))
    except KeyError:
        return fallback
@

Some callers must tell a value the lecturer set from the built-in
default it falls back to: the slide helpers record the locale of a
slide only when someone chose it (\cref{sec:recorded-locale}), and the
built-in [[en]] is nobody's choice.  [[configured_setting]] therefore
returns [[None]] for an unset key instead of the default.

<<functions>>=
def configured_setting(key: str) -> Any:
    """Return the value set for ``key`` in the configuration, or ``None``."""

    full_key = normalize_config_key(key)
    try:
        return _coerce_setting_value(full_key, load_config().get(full_key))
    except KeyError:
        return None
@

<<test functions>>=
def test_configured_setting_tells_a_set_value_from_the_default(
    tmp_path, monkeypatch
):
    monkeypatch.setenv("MENTIPY_CONFIG", str(tmp_path / "config.json"))
    assert get_setting(LOCALE_CONFIG) == DEFAULT_LOCALE
    assert configured_setting(LOCALE_CONFIG) is None

    set_setting("locale", "en")
    assert configured_setting(LOCALE_CONFIG) == "en"
@

Writing a setting uses the same normalisation path as reading, so the CLI can
remain concise without creating a second spelling of each configuration key on
disk.

<<functions>>=
def set_setting(key: str, value: Any):
    """Store ``value`` under ``key`` in the mentipy configuration."""

    config = load_config(writeback=True)
    config.set(normalize_config_key(key), value)
@

Listing settings reports the effective values rather than only the values
present in the JSON file.  The CLI now relies on [[typerconf]]'s generic tree
printer, but this helper still gives tests and internal callers one stable view
of Mentipy's built-in defaults, including nullable settings that may not yet be
present on disk.

<<functions>>=
def list_settings() -> dict[str, Any]:
    """Return the effective values of the built-in mentipy settings."""

    return {
        full_key.split(".", 1)[1]: get_setting(full_key, default)
        for full_key, default in DEFAULT_SETTINGS.items()
    }
@

When a config value arrives as text, we first try JSON decoding and only fall
back to the original string when decoding fails.  That is enough to recover the
typed built-in settings from [[typerconf]]'s generic [[--set]] interface.

<<functions>>=
def parse_config_value(raw: str) -> Any:
    """Interpret ``raw`` as JSON when possible, else keep it as text."""

    try:
        return json.loads(raw)
    except json.JSONDecodeError:
        return raw
@

\section{Base directory}
\label{sec:base-directory}

A slide deck names its files relative to its own directory:
[[Store("mentipy.json")]], [[qr_dir="mentipy-obj"]], the image of a
pin question.  That works as long as PythonTeX runs in the deck's
directory.  It stops working when several decks are bound into one book
with the [[import]] package: every chapter shares one PythonTeX session,
which starts in the book's directory, so the deck's relative paths would
land next to the book instead of next to the deck.  The book could
[[os.chdir]] the whole session into each chapter, but that moves the
ground under every other package sharing the session.

We instead give Mentipy a \emph{base directory}: when set, every file
Mentipy reads or writes on the author's behalf is resolved against it.
The paths Mentipy \emph{emits into LaTeX} do not change.  Those stay
exactly as the author wrote them, because the [[import]] package already
adds the deck's directory to [[\input@path]] and [[\Ginput@path]], so
[[\input{mentipy-obj/Q-ab12.pdf_tex}]] finds the file in the deck either
way.  Resolving only the file-system side is what lets one deck compile
both standalone and inside the book without edits.

The companion package [[mentipy.sty]] (\cref{chap:sty}) sets the base
directory from LaTeX, including an [[auto]] mode that follows
[[\import@path]].  The functions here are the Python half and work on
their own too.

\subsection{Anchoring a relative base directory}

A relative base directory needs an anchor.  The current working directory
is the wrong one: the reason we need a base directory at all is that
something may have changed the working directory behind our back.  We
anchor at the working directory in force when this module is first
imported, which for a PythonTeX document is the directory the session
started in.  The didactic package anchors its [[\runbasedir]] the same
way, so a book can hand both packages the same relative path and they
agree on where it points.  The companion LaTeX package imports Mentipy
while the preamble loads, which pins the anchor before any chapter
runs; a document using only the Python side should import Mentipy early
for the same reason.  An absolute base directory ignores the anchor.

<<constants>>=
_ANCHOR_DIR = Path.cwd()
@

\subsection{Setting the base directory}

The base directory is module state rather than a parameter, because the
deck and the book are written by different hands: the deck's own
PythonTeX code builds its store and its helper arguments, while only the
book knows that the deck has been moved.  A module-level setting lets
the book change where the deck's files go without touching the deck.

We keep [[None]] as \enquote{no base directory}, which leaves relative
paths relative, exactly as Mentipy behaved before this setting existed.
The empty string means the same thing, because that is what the LaTeX
side produces for [[auto]] outside any [[\subimport]], and what the
didactic package's [[\runbasedir{}]] means.  We deliberately do not
call [[Path.resolve()]] on the stored value: joining the anchor and the
relative path is enough to make it absolute, and leaving symbolic links
unresolved keeps the path the author wrote recognisable in error
messages.

<<constants>>=
_base_dir: Path | None = None
<<functions>>=
def set_base_dir(path: str | Path | None) -> None:
    """Resolve Mentipy's files against `path` from now on.

    A relative `path` is anchored at the directory the session started
    in (the working directory when Mentipy was first imported), not at
    the current working directory.  `None` or `""` switches the base
    directory off, so relative paths resolve against the current
    working directory as usual.  Paths emitted into LaTeX are never
    affected.
    """

    global _base_dir
    if path is None or str(path) == "":
        _base_dir = None
    else:
        _base_dir = _ANCHOR_DIR / Path(path)


def get_base_dir() -> Path | None:
    """Return the absolute base directory, or `None` when it is off."""

    return _base_dir
@

Scripts and tests want the setting for one block of code and the old
value back afterwards, even when the block raises.  A context manager
states that directly.

<<functions>>=
@contextmanager
def base_dir(path: str | Path | None) -> Iterator[Path | None]:
    """Set the base directory for the duration of a `with` block."""

    global _base_dir
    previous = _base_dir
    set_base_dir(path)
    try:
        yield _base_dir
    finally:
        _base_dir = previous
@

\subsection{Resolving a path}

Every call site that touches the file system passes its path through
[[resolve_path]].  Absolute paths pass through unchanged, so an author
who spells out a full path always gets that path.  With the base
directory off, relative paths also pass through unchanged, which keeps
the historical behaviour byte for byte: they resolve against whatever
the working directory is at the moment of the actual file operation.

<<functions>>=
def resolve_path(path: str | Path) -> Path:
    """Return `path` resolved against the base directory, if one is set."""

    candidate = Path(path)
    if _base_dir is None or candidate.is_absolute():
        return candidate
    return _base_dir / candidate
@

The tests pin the anchor to a temporary directory and restore the module
state afterwards, so no test leaks a base directory into the next one.

