\section{Store file primitives}

The empty-store template is copied on demand rather than reused directly, so a
caller who mutates the returned structure cannot accidentally mutate the module
constant used for the next missing file.

<<functions>>=
def _blank_store() -> dict[str, Any]:
    return {
        "questions": dict(STORE_TEMPLATE["questions"]),
        "answers": dict(STORE_TEMPLATE["answers"]),
    }


_STORE_LOCKS: dict[Path, threading.Lock] = {}
_STORE_LOCKS_GUARD = threading.Lock()


def _store_lock(path: Path) -> threading.Lock:
    resolved = path.resolve()
    with _STORE_LOCKS_GUARD:
        lock = _STORE_LOCKS.get(resolved)
        if lock is None:
            lock = threading.Lock()
            _STORE_LOCKS[resolved] = lock
    return lock
@

Answer timestamps are stored as UTC ISO strings with a [[Z]] suffix.  That is a
small readability concession for the JSON file while still being unambiguous
for later time-window filtering.

<<functions>>=
def _timestamp() -> str:
    return datetime.now(timezone.utc).isoformat().replace("+00:00", "Z")
@

Reading a timestamp back is the inverse.  [[datetime.fromisoformat]]
only learnt to parse the [[Z]] suffix in Python~3.11, so rather than
raise the supported Python floor or pull in [[dateutil]], the parser
substitutes [[+00:00]] back, undoing what the writer did.  Comparisons
must go through the parsed value, never the strings: [[isoformat]] drops
the microseconds when they are zero, so the string [[...00Z]] sorts
after [[...00.000001Z]] although it is earlier.  A naive value, from a
hand-edited file, is taken as UTC.

<<functions>>=
def parse_timestamp(timestamp: str) -> datetime:
    """Return the stored UTC ``timestamp`` as an aware datetime."""

    moment = datetime.fromisoformat(timestamp.replace("Z", "+00:00"))
    if moment.tzinfo is None:
        moment = moment.replace(tzinfo=timezone.utc)
    return moment
@

Humans read answer logs in the room's local time, not in the storage time zone.
Formatting is therefore a separate public helper: stored data remains stable,
while every human-facing renderer can use the same local-time presentation.

<<functions>>=
def format_timestamp(timestamp: str) -> str:
    """Return ``timestamp`` formatted in the local timezone."""

    moment = parse_timestamp(timestamp)
    return moment.astimezone().strftime("%Y-%m-%d %H:%M")
@

The formatter should use the process-local time zone.  The test fixes the
process time zone to a UTC+2 rule so the expected clock time is independent of
where the test machine happens to run.

<<test functions>>=
def test_format_timestamp_uses_local_time(monkeypatch):
    if not hasattr(time, "tzset"):
        pytest.skip("tzset is unavailable on this platform")

    original_tz = os.environ.get("TZ")
    monkeypatch.setenv("TZ", "UTC-2")
    time.tzset()

    try:
        assert format_timestamp("2026-05-12T11:30:45Z") == "2026-05-12 13:30"
    finally:
        if original_tz is None:
            monkeypatch.delenv("TZ", raising=False)
        else:
            monkeypatch.setenv("TZ", original_tz)
        time.tzset()
@

\section{Persistent store}

The store itself is small, but the class boundary matters: the HTTP server, the
LaTeX integration, and the CLI can all share the same persistence rules.

<<classes>>=
class Store:
    """Persist questions and answers in a JSON file."""

    def __init__(self, path: str | Path | None = None, min_prefix: int | None = None):
        chosen_path = path or get_setting(STORE_CONFIG, DEFAULT_STORE_PATH)
        chosen_prefix = min_prefix
        if chosen_prefix is None:
            chosen_prefix = int(
                get_setting(MIN_PREFIX_CONFIG, DEFAULT_MIN_PREFIX)
            )

        self.path = resolve_path(chosen_path)
        self.min_prefix = int(chosen_prefix)
        self._resolved_path = self.path.resolve()
        self._lock = _store_lock(self._resolved_path)

    <<store methods>>
@

The store path goes through [[resolve_path]] once, at construction.
A deck builds its [[Store]] in its own PythonTeX code while the book
has the deck's base directory in force, and the object must keep
pointing at the deck's store after the book moves on to the next
chapter.  The image directory derives from [[self.path]], so it follows
without further work.  The lock is keyed by the fully resolved path, so
that two [[Store]] objects for one file share it, and so is the
registration run of \cref{sec:live-deck}; resolving once, here, keeps the
two keys equal even if the working directory changes later.

<<test functions>>=
def test_store_resolves_its_path_against_the_base_dir(
    anchor, tmp_path, monkeypatch
):
    monkeypatch.chdir(anchor)
    set_base_dir("../deck")

    store = Store("mentipy.json", min_prefix=4)
    store.register(OpenQuestion("Comment?"))
    store.save_image(b"png bytes")
    set_base_dir(None)

    assert store.path == anchor / "../deck/mentipy.json"
    assert (tmp_path / "deck" / "mentipy.json").exists()
    assert (tmp_path / "deck" / "mentipy-images").is_dir()
    assert not (anchor / "mentipy.json").exists()
@

Reading the store on every operation is the simplest way to make the server see
newly compiled questions without introducing cache invalidation.  Missing files
are treated as empty stores, and older or hand-written files are normalised by
adding the top-level collections the rest of the class expects.

<<store methods>>=
def _data(self) -> dict[str, Any]:
    if not self.path.exists():
        return _blank_store()

    with self.path.open(encoding="utf-8") as handle:
        data = json.load(handle)

    data.setdefault("questions", {})
    data.setdefault("answers", {})
    return data
@

Writes go through a sibling temporary file and then [[os.replace]].  The
temporary file may be incomplete if the process dies mid-write, but it never
becomes the real store unless the final replace succeeds.  We dump without
[[sort_keys]] because the order of the [[questions]] dict is now load-bearing:
it encodes the sequence in which questions were last registered, which the
\enquote{Next $\rightarrow$} navigation walks.  Sorting on disk would erase that
information.  Python's JSON library preserves dict insertion order on dump and
recovers it on load, so the round-trip carries the sequence faithfully.
Every write also tidies the remembered QR targets
(\cref{sec:remembered-qr-artifacts}): it migrates the lists older
versions of Mentipy wrote, so a store is migrated by whatever first
writes it after an upgrade, and it drops targets outside the checkout
whose directory has gone.

<<store methods>>=
def _write(self, data: dict[str, Any]):
    self._tidy_qr_targets(data)
    self.path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    temp_path = self.path.with_name(f"{self.path.name}.tmp")

    with temp_path.open("w", encoding="utf-8") as handle:
        json.dump(data, handle, ensure_ascii=False, indent=2)
        handle.write("\n")

    os.replace(temp_path, self.path)
@

