\subsection{The live deck}
\label{sec:live-deck}

The store keeps every question it has ever registered, because old QR
codes and old answers must keep working after a slide is dropped.  The
navigation row must nevertheless follow the deck as it is now, so
something in the store must mark which questions the last build
registered, and in which order.

A build is one or more PythonTeX runs, and each run is a fresh Python
process that executes the deck's code in document order, registering each
question as it goes.  The run is therefore the natural unit, and the
process is its boundary: a process that has registered nothing in a store
yet is at the start of a run there.  Each registration records, under
[[registered_after]], the hash of the question registered just before it
in this process, or an explicit [[null]] for the run's first question.
The live sequence is the chain of those links followed back from the
store's last entry to the [[null]].  A dropped question is no longer
registered, so nothing in the new chain points to it; and since
[[register]] never touches an entry it is not registering, the orphan's
keys and answers stay exactly as they were.

The links replace a registration serial drawn from an ever-growing counter
in the store, whose live sequence was the run of consecutive serials at
the end of the dict.  That design had two flaws, both visible in a course
repository that commits a deck's store because the store holds the
lecture's answers.  First, the store was input and output of the build and
never reached a fixed point: every clean build of an unchanged deck
rewrote every live question's serial and the counter, so the committed
file changed on every book build and in every fresh worktree.  A link
depends only on the order of the deck, so an unchanged rebuild writes the
same values and leaves the file byte-identical.  Second, consecutive
serials cannot tell a new run from the tail of the previous one.  With one
PythonTeX run per build, a deck that drops its last question re-registers
the others with serials that continue straight from the dropped one,
which therefore stays live; a whole new deck registered into an old store
continues the old deck's serials in the same way.  A second run per build
hid both cases, because its serials continue from the first run instead.
The [[null]] at the head of each run is the boundary that the serials
lacked.

The key is [[registered_after]] rather than a bare [[previous]] because
questions already carry a [[next]] field, the lecturer's opt-out from the
\enquote{Next $\rightarrow$} link, and a [[previous]] beside it would read
as its counterpart.  [[registered_after]] says what it records, and
[[null]] reads as \enquote{after nothing}: the head of a run.  The
[[null]] is written explicitly so that \enquote{first in its run} differs
from a missing key, which marks an entry written before the links
existed.

<<constants>>=
REGISTRATION_SERIAL_KEY = "registration_serial"
REGISTERED_AFTER_KEY = "registered_after"
@

\paragraph{One run per store.}
The process's memory of its runs maps a store's path, resolved at
construction as for the store's lock, to the hash last registered there,
and it is empty at import.  It is keyed by store because one process can
write several stores: a book binds several decks into one document, and
its single PythonTeX process registers each deck's questions in the deck's
own store.  A single process-wide \enquote{last question} would link the
first question of one deck to the last question of the deck before it, a
hash from another store, and two decks registering alternately, or sharing
a question, would tangle their chains.  [[register]] reads and writes the
map under the store's lock, so the link it writes and the run's memory
change together.

<<functions>>=
_LAST_REGISTERED: dict[Path, str] = {}
@

A process can also say where its run starts, which matters for the one
writer whose process is not a run: a [[mentipy render]] registers a single
question, and a deck built one render per question must still form one
chain (\cref{sec:what-counts-as-a-run}).  Starting a run forgets the
process's last registration in the store.  Continuing one takes the
store's last entry as that last registration, so the next question links
to it with its serial plus one, as if this process had registered both.
In a process that has registered in the store already, the last entry is
that registration anyway, unless another process wrote in between, and
then following what the store holds last is the better choice.  Both are
methods of the store, not flags of [[register]], because they are about
the process rather than about one question: the CLI says once, before it
renders, what kind of run its single registration belongs to.

<<store methods>>=
def start_registration_run(self):
    """Make the next registration in this process start a new run."""

    with self._lock:
        _LAST_REGISTERED.pop(self._resolved_path, None)


def continue_registration_run(self):
    """Make the next registration continue the store's latest run.

    The next question follows the store's last question, as if this
    process had registered that one too.
    """

    with self._lock:
        questions = self._data()["questions"]
        if questions:
            _LAST_REGISTERED[self._resolved_path] = next(reversed(questions))
@

Tests also start new runs, because they model several builds in one
pytest process; one that models a fresh process forgets the runs of
every store.

<<functions>>=
def _start_registration_run(store: Store | None = None):
    """Start a new registration run in `store`, or in every store."""

    if store is None:
        _LAST_REGISTERED.clear()
        return
    store.start_registration_run()
@

\paragraph{Choosing the link.}
Usually the link is the run's last registration and the serial is one
more than that question's serial.  Two cases differ.  A question
registered twice in a row in one run would link to itself, and the walk
would stop at it at once, leaving it the only live question; it keeps the
link and serial it has.  And the run's last registration may have left the
store since, when the file was replaced between two code blocks; a link to
it would end the walk anyway, but writing [[null]] keeps the link and the
serial consistent: the question starts a run.

<<choose the link and serial for [[full_hash]]>>=
questions = data["questions"]
previous = _LAST_REGISTERED.get(self._resolved_path)
if previous == full_hash and full_hash in questions:
    previous = questions[full_hash].get(REGISTERED_AFTER_KEY)
    serial = int(questions[full_hash].get(REGISTRATION_SERIAL_KEY, 1))
elif previous in questions:
    serial = int(questions[previous].get(REGISTRATION_SERIAL_KEY, 0)) + 1
else:
    previous = None
    serial = 1
@

\paragraph{Serials for older readers.}
A [[mentipy serve]] from before the links, on another machine or pinned in
a course repository, reads the same file and still finds its live
sequence from the serials.  We keep writing serials for it, but
deterministic ones: a question's position in its run, one more than its
predecessor's and 1 for the head.  The old rule walks back from the last
question while each serial is one less than the one after it.  When the
chain of links ends at a [[null]], its head has serial~1 and no question
has serial~0, so the old rule stops exactly where the chain does; the
tests check that for every way of editing a deck below.  A deck that
asks the same question twice is the exception.  Its chain ends at the
repeat (see below), at a head with a serial above one, and the old rule
walks on past it as long as the serials keep falling by one: building
[Dropped?] and then [A?, B?, A?] leaves Dropped?, B? and A? with serials
1, 2 and 3 in that order, and an old reader shows all three where the
links show B? and A?.  The counter that used to feed the serials is no
longer written.  Older readers recompute it on every read as the largest
serial, so new stores leave it out, and an old store's counter stays in
the file untouched.

\paragraph{Checking the links against the serials.}
The other direction needs a guard.  An older Mentipy that builds into a
store with links copies each question's [[registered_after]] unchanged,
as store-owned metadata, and gives the question the next value of its
counter.  Followed blindly, those stale links lead through the questions
of the last current build, so a question that the older build dropped
would stay live.  The walk therefore trusts a link only where the
serials agree with it, and reads the store by the serial rule as soon as
they do not.  Two checks suffice.  Every step to a predecessor that the
walk follows needs the predecessor's serial to be one less than the
successor's.  And a chain that ends at a [[null]], or at an entry
without the key, needs its head to have serial~1.

The current writer passes both by construction: it writes the serial as
the predecessor's plus one and as 1 at a [[null]].  An older writer
fails one of them once the walk reaches what it wrote, because its
serials continue above every serial in the store.  A stale link from one
of its questions to a question the current writer left points to a
question whose serial is one less than the linking question's serial
was, which was at most the store's largest serial; the older writer's
new serial is above that, so the step fails.  A chain made only of its
own questions ends at a link it copied: at a stale [[null]], whose head
has a serial above one, or at one of the current writer's questions,
which is the first case.  The one end the checks cannot catch is a
repeat: a cycle among the copied links, which a deck that repeats a
question or a render that continued a run left behind, followed by an
older build that registered the cycle's questions in the order of its
links.  A simulation of random histories found such a store after 3 of
about 1900 older builds, each time after a continued render.  When a
check fails, the serial rule is exactly what the older version shows
itself, and the next current build writes links that agree again.

The head check is limited to a chain that ends at a [[null]] on
purpose.  A chain that ends at a repeat has a head that is not its run's
first registration, from a deck that asks a question twice or from a
render that continued an earlier run (\cref{sec:what-counts-as-a-run}),
so its serial is above one although the chain is right; demanding
serial~1 there would discard every such deck.

\paragraph{Following the links.}
The walk starts at the store's last entry, which is always live: whatever
registered last ran last.  It ends at a [[null]], at a hash the store does
not hold, at anything that is not a hash, and at a hash it has already
visited.  Cycles are not only hand edits.  A deck that asks the same
question twice, A, B, A, moves A to the end with a link to B, while B still
links to A; and a process that registers a deck twice without a run
boundary links the second run's head to the first run's tail.  Stopping at
the first repeat leaves a repeated question live at its last position
only and drops whatever came before its first occurrence: one entry per
hash cannot hold two positions, and the serials lost the same questions.

<<functions>>=
def _live_hashes(data: dict[str, Any]) -> list[str]:
    questions = data["questions"]
    if not questions:
        return []

    last = next(reversed(questions))
    if REGISTERED_AFTER_KEY not in questions[last]:
        return _live_hashes_by_serials(questions)

    live = [last]
    previous = questions[last][REGISTERED_AFTER_KEY]
    while (
        isinstance(previous, str)
        and previous in questions
        and previous not in live
    ):
        if not _serial_follows(questions[previous], questions[live[-1]]):
            return _live_hashes_by_serials(questions)
        live.append(previous)
        previous = questions[previous].get(REGISTERED_AFTER_KEY)

    if previous is None and questions[live[-1]].get(REGISTRATION_SERIAL_KEY) != 1:
        return _live_hashes_by_serials(questions)

    live.reverse()
    return live


def _serial_follows(before: dict[str, Any], after: dict[str, Any]) -> bool:
    first = before.get(REGISTRATION_SERIAL_KEY)
    second = after.get(REGISTRATION_SERIAL_KEY)
    return isinstance(first, int) and isinstance(second, int) and (
        first + 1 == second
    )
@

The membership test on the list is linear, which is fine for a lecture's
worth of questions and keeps the walk's state to the one list it returns.
A serial that is missing or not a number fails the check, so a
hand-edited chain is read by the serial rule rather than trusted.

A last entry without the key at all was written before the links existed,
so the store is read by the serial rule it was written for, and an old
store navigates exactly as before until its first build writes links.  The
rule is kept as it was, including its reading of a question without a
serial, from before the serials, as having its position in the store as
its serial, which makes such a store a single run.  That position is
computed on read and never written back: writing it would rewrite every
orphan of such a store on its next write.

<<functions>>=
def _live_hashes_by_serials(questions: dict[str, Any]) -> list[str]:
    serials = [
        (full_hash, int(meta.get(REGISTRATION_SERIAL_KEY, position)))
        for position, (full_hash, meta) in enumerate(questions.items(), start=1)
    ]
    if not serials:
        return []

    live = [serials[-1][0]]
    expected = serials[-1][1]

    for full_hash, serial in reversed(serials[:-1]):
        if serial + 1 != expected:
            break
        live.append(full_hash)
        expected = serial

    live.reverse()
    return live
@

\paragraph{What counts as a run.}
\label{sec:what-counts-as-a-run}
The process boundary is right for PythonTeX, whose run registers a whole
deck.  A [[mentipy render]] is a process too, but it registers one
question, and a deck built one render per question, from a Makefile
say, would make every question a run of its own and leave only the last
one live, where the serials kept such a deck whole.  The CLI therefore
continues the store's latest run before it registers
([[continue_registration_run]] above): the rendered question follows the
store's last question with the next serial, which is what the serials
did.  A render-built deck thus forms one chain, and a single render of
an existing question from a terminal shows what it showed under the
serials: the question moves to the end and links to the old last
question, and the walk back from it stops where it meets the question's
old successor, whose link points to the moved question.

Continuing also keeps the serials' weaknesses.  A render-built deck that
is rendered again appends to its own old chain: the store changes on
every build, the walk ends at the repeat of the deck's first question
rather than at a [[null]], and a question dropped from the end of the
deck stays live.  [[mentipy render --new-run]] starts a run instead
([[start_registration_run]]).  A Makefile passes it to the deck's first
question, whose [[null]] then cuts the chain from the old build, so a
render-built deck settles as one built by PythonTeX does.  The
[[mentipy.latex]] helpers do neither, because their process is the run.

Finally, a deck that registers its questions from several named
PythonTeX sessions gets one process per session, so only the session
that ran last is live; [[mentipy.sty]] already assumes the default
session (\cref{chap:sty}).

\subsubsection{Testing the live deck}

The tests model a build the way it happens: one or two PythonTeX runs,
each a fresh process registering the deck's questions in document order.
Inside one pytest process a fresh process is [[_start_registration_run]],
and the tests call it explicitly at every run boundary, because which
registrations share a run is exactly what they are about.  They read the
live sequence back through the public navigation helpers rather than
through [[_live_hashes]], walking from the last registered question back
to the head and then forward again, so a disagreement between
[[prev_question]] and [[next_question]] shows up too.

<<test functions>>=
def _build(store_path, deck, *, runs=1):
    """Register `deck` as one build does: `runs` fresh PythonTeX runs."""

    store = Store(store_path, min_prefix=4)
    for _ in range(runs):
        _start_registration_run(store)
        for question in deck:
            store.register(question)
    return store


def _live_sequence(store):
    rows = store.all_questions()
    if not rows:
        return []
    head = rows[-1]["hash"]
    while (before := store.prev_question(head)) is not None:
        head = before
    live = [head]
    while (after := store.next_question(live[-1])) is not None:
        live.append(after)
    return live


def _hashes(deck):
    return [question_hash(question) for question in deck]


def _deck(*texts):
    return [OpenQuestion(text) for text in texts]
@

The property the issue was about comes first: a store that holds a
lecture's answers is committed next to the deck, and a clean rebuild of
the unchanged deck must leave it alone.  The comparison is on the file's
bytes, not on parsed dicts, because dict equality ignores the order of
the questions, which is the one thing the navigation depends on.

<<test functions>>=
@pytest.mark.parametrize("runs", [1, 2])
def test_an_unchanged_rebuild_leaves_the_store_byte_identical(tmp_path, runs):
    path = tmp_path / "mentipy.json"
    deck = _deck("Q1", "Q2", "Q3", "Q4", "Q5")
    store = _build(path, deck, runs=runs)
    store.record(question_hash(deck[1]), "an answer")
    built = path.read_bytes()

    _build(path, deck, runs=runs)

    assert path.read_bytes() == built
    assert _live_sequence(store) == _hashes(deck)
@

Every way of editing a deck must leave exactly the new deck live, in its
order, with or without a second PythonTeX run.  The dropped questions
must not be touched at all: their entries keep their keys in their order
(compared as serialised JSON, which preserves order) and their answers,
nothing live links to them, and they have no neighbours.  Finally the
store must settle: one more unchanged build is byte-identical.  The
serials of the previous design failed the last check in every scenario,
and with one run per build they also kept a dropped last question, and a
whole old deck, live.

<<test functions>>=
DECK_CHANGES = {
    "drop the first": ("Q2", "Q3", "Q4", "Q5"),
    "drop a middle one": ("Q1", "Q2", "Q4", "Q5"),
    "drop the last": ("Q1", "Q2", "Q3", "Q4"),
    "add one in the middle": ("Q1", "Q2", "New", "Q3", "Q4", "Q5"),
    "swap two": ("Q1", "Q2", "Q4", "Q3", "Q5"),
    "a whole new deck": ("N1", "N2", "N3"),
}


@pytest.mark.parametrize("runs", [1, 2])
@pytest.mark.parametrize("change", list(DECK_CHANGES))
def test_a_changed_deck_becomes_the_live_sequence(tmp_path, change, runs):
    path = tmp_path / "mentipy.json"
    old_deck = _deck("Q1", "Q2", "Q3", "Q4", "Q5")
    store = _build(path, old_deck, runs=runs)
    for full_hash in _hashes(old_deck):
        store.record(full_hash, f"answer to {full_hash[:4]}")
    before = json.loads(path.read_text())

    new_deck = _deck(*DECK_CHANGES[change])
    _build(path, new_deck, runs=runs)
    rebuilt = path.read_bytes()
    after = json.loads(rebuilt)

    live = _hashes(new_deck)
    dropped = set(before["questions"]) - set(live)
    assert _live_sequence(store) == live
    for full_hash in live:
        assert after["questions"][full_hash][REGISTERED_AFTER_KEY] not in dropped
    for full_hash in dropped:
        assert json.dumps(after["questions"][full_hash]) == json.dumps(
            before["questions"][full_hash]
        )
        assert after["answers"][full_hash] == before["answers"][full_hash]
        assert store.next_question(full_hash) is None
        assert store.prev_question(full_hash) is None

    _build(path, new_deck, runs=runs)
    assert path.read_bytes() == rebuilt
@

The book is one PythonTeX process that registers questions in several
decks' stores.  Registering them alternately is the sharpest form of that:
a single process-wide \enquote{last question} would link each question to
one in the other store, which the walk cannot follow, and leave only one
question live per store.

<<test functions>>=
def test_stores_registered_in_one_process_do_not_link_to_each_other(tmp_path):
    first = Store(tmp_path / "helloworld" / "mentipy.json", min_prefix=4)
    second = Store(tmp_path / "recap" / "mentipy.json", min_prefix=4)
    first_deck = _deck("Hello?", "World?", "Again?")
    second_deck = _deck("Recap one?", "Recap two?", "Recap three?")

    _start_registration_run()
    for one, other in zip(first_deck, second_deck):
        first.register(one)
        second.register(other)

    assert _live_sequence(first) == _hashes(first_deck)
    assert _live_sequence(second) == _hashes(second_deck)
@

A store written before the links existed must navigate exactly as it did,
until the first build gives it links; and that build must leave its
orphans and its old top-level counter alone.  The legacy store here is
the file Mentipy~0.16 wrote for a deck of four questions built once and
then rebuilt without its third question: the orphan keeps serial 3, the
rebuild's run has serials 5 to 7, and the counter stands at 7.  We make
it by building with the current code and rewriting the file into that
shape, rather than by hand-writing the JSON, so every other key is what a
real registration writes.

<<test functions>>=
def _as_written_before_links(path, serials, counter):
    data = json.loads(path.read_text())
    for full_hash, meta in data["questions"].items():
        del meta[REGISTERED_AFTER_KEY]
        meta[REGISTRATION_SERIAL_KEY] = serials[full_hash]
    data["registration_counter"] = counter
    path.write_text(json.dumps(data, ensure_ascii=False, indent=2) + "\n")


def test_a_store_without_links_navigates_by_serials_until_rebuilt(tmp_path):
    path = tmp_path / "mentipy.json"
    q1, q2, q3, q4 = deck = _deck("Q1", "Q2", "Q3", "Q4")
    store = _build(path, deck)
    _build(path, [q1, q2, q4])
    h1, h2, h3, h4 = _hashes(deck)
    _as_written_before_links(path, {h3: 3, h1: 5, h2: 6, h4: 7}, counter=7)
    legacy = json.loads(path.read_text())

    assert _live_sequence(store) == [h1, h2, h4]

    _build(path, [q1, q2, q4])
    rebuilt = path.read_bytes()
    after = json.loads(rebuilt)

    assert _live_sequence(store) == [h1, h2, h4]
    assert after["questions"][h1][REGISTERED_AFTER_KEY] is None
    assert json.dumps(after["questions"][h3]) == json.dumps(
        legacy["questions"][h3]
    )
    assert after["registration_counter"] == 7
    _build(path, [q1, q2, q4])
    assert path.read_bytes() == rebuilt
@

Older stores still, from before the serials, have neither key on any
question.  Mentipy~0.16 gave each such question its position as a serial,
which makes the whole store one run; the fallback keeps that reading
without writing those serials into the file.

<<test functions>>=
def test_a_store_without_serials_is_one_live_run(tmp_path):
    path = tmp_path / "mentipy.json"
    deck = _deck("Q1", "Q2", "Q3")
    store = _build(path, deck)
    data = json.loads(path.read_text())
    for meta in data["questions"].values():
        del meta[REGISTERED_AFTER_KEY]
        del meta[REGISTRATION_SERIAL_KEY]
    path.write_text(json.dumps(data, indent=2) + "\n")

    assert _live_sequence(store) == _hashes(deck)
    assert REGISTRATION_SERIAL_KEY not in store._data()["questions"][
        question_hash(deck[0])
    ]
