Prefix iteration is a small helper for server startup checks.  It takes a
snapshot under the lock and yields afterwards, avoiding a long-held lock around
caller code that merely consumes strings.

<<store methods>>=
def iter_prefixes(self) -> Iterator[str]:
    """Yield the URL prefix of every registered question."""

    with self._lock:
        data = self._data()

    for meta in data["questions"].values():
        yield meta["url_prefix"]
@

\subsection{Remembered QR artifacts}
\label{sec:remembered-qr-artifacts}

QR regeneration needs one extra piece of operational metadata that should not
change the question's identity: the concrete files earlier renders wrote.  The
same question can appear in more than one document directory or image format,
so we remember a de-duplicated list of those QR targets beside the canonical
question payload.

How to spell such a path is the design question.  A path relative to the
working directory will not do, because a later [[mentipy serve]] may run
from anywhere and must still refresh the same files.  Older versions of
Mentipy stored absolute paths, and those fail as soon as the store
travels.  Some projects track [[mentipy.json]] in git, so that every
clone serves the same prefixes.  With absolute paths the store then
carries one machine's home directory into every clone, and a build in a
git worktree records the worktree's paths, after which [[mentipy serve]]
in the main checkout writes QR codes into the worktree.

\paragraph{Two keys.}

We keep the entries this version writes under a new key, [[qr_paths]],
and read the old key, [[qr_targets]], only to migrate it.  The separate
key is what lets the rest of this section decide how far to trust an
entry by where it is stored instead of guessing from its shape.  An
entry under [[qr_paths]] was written by the rules below, so it means
what it says.  An entry under [[qr_targets]] is an absolute path from
some checkout on some machine, perhaps this one, perhaps a worktree
that has since been removed, perhaps a colleague's laptop, and nothing
in the entry itself tells which.  Both keys are auxiliary: hidden from
[[metadata()]], kept out of the hash, and carried over by
refresh-on-merge like every key that is not part of the question's own
payload.

<<constants>>=
QR_PATHS_KEY = "qr_paths"
@

\paragraph{Checkouts.}

What a clone does preserve is the layout inside the checkout.  A
checkout is recognised by the [[.git]] entry at its root: a directory in
a clone, a file in a worktree or a submodule.  The store's checkout is
the nearest directory, from the store's directory upwards, that has
one.  Asking the file system rather than running [[git]] keeps the
store free of a subprocess on every render.  Outside version control
there is no checkout to go by, and the parent of the store's directory
stands in for it, so that a deck and a handout directory beside it
still travel together when the parent is copied.

A checkout can contain other checkouts, and they come in two sorts.
A worktree under [[.claude/worktrees]] of a repository whose store sits
at its root is another copy of this project: it carries its own copy
of the tracked store, and its files are that copy's business.  A
submodule, or a course repository below a central store that is not
under version control, is part of this checkout's layout instead: it
does not carry the store, and it travels with the checkout.  We tell
them apart by exactly that difference.  Walking up from a path, the
first [[.git]] below the store's checkout root marks a nested
checkout, and it is another copy of the project when it holds the
store at the same place relative to its root as the store's checkout
does.

Every probe goes through [[os.path.exists]] and [[os.path.isdir]]
rather than their [[pathlib]] counterparts.  The paths probed include
old stores' paths, perhaps in another user's home on a shared machine,
and up to Python 3.11 [[Path.exists]] raises [[PermissionError]] for a
directory it may not enter, where the [[os.path]] functions answer
[[False]].

<<classes>>=
class _Checkout(NamedTuple):
    root: Path
    store: Path
<<functions>>=
def _checkout_of(store_file: Path) -> _Checkout:
    directory = store_file.parent
    for candidate in (directory, *directory.parents):
        if os.path.exists(candidate / ".git"):
            return _Checkout(candidate, store_file.relative_to(candidate))
    root = directory.parent
    return _Checkout(root, store_file.relative_to(root))


def _in_other_copy(path: Path, checkout: _Checkout) -> bool:
    for directory in path.parents:
        if directory == checkout.root:
            return False
        if os.path.exists(directory / ".git"):
            return os.path.exists(directory / checkout.store)
    return False
@

The store's directory is the one the store file is read from, resolved
when it is needed.  [[self.path]] went through [[resolve_path]] at
construction (\cref{sec:base-directory}), so under a base directory it
is already absolute; without one it may be relative and then names the
store in the current working directory, which is exactly the file the
next read or write will use.  Resolving it the same way when we write
an entry and when we read one back is what keeps the two in agreement.

<<store methods>>=
def _store_dir(self) -> Path:
    return self.path.parent.resolve()


def _checkout(self) -> _Checkout:
    return _checkout_of(self._store_dir() / self.path.name)
@

\paragraph{Recording a target.}

A new target is made absolute against the working directory, as
before, because that is what the caller's path means.  Where it lies
then decides how it is recorded.

\begin{description}
\item[In the store's checkout] it is recorded relative to the store's
  directory, in POSIX form so the entry reads the same on every
  platform.  That covers the deck's QR directory next to the store,
  and also a handout directory beside the deck's directory, which the
  entry reaches by climbing out of the store's directory with [[..]]:
  inside a repository the sibling layout is the same in every clone.
  The spelling is lexical ([[os.path.relpath]]), which is safe because
  both paths are resolved first, and it cannot fail on the different
  drives of Windows, since the target shares the checkout's drive.
\item[In another copy of the project] nested in the checkout, it is
  not recorded at all.  It is that copy's file, and that copy's own
  store records it.  A relative entry would spread
  [[.claude/worktrees/x/...]] through git to every clone, and an
  absolute one would make this checkout's server rewrite the other
  copy's QR codes.
\item[Anywhere else] it is recorded by its absolute path.  This is a
  target the author chose deliberately outside the checkout: a
  handout directory in a sibling repository, a printout directory, or
  a deck in its own repository whose store is a central one configured
  with [[mentipy.store]].  A relative spelling would make every clone
  write, and create, a directory outside itself.
\end{description}

The price of relative entries is a store moved alone, away from its
decks, which then names files that are not there; that is a rarer case
than a store that travels with its checkout.

Recognising another copy by the store it carries has one blind spot.
A store at the root of its checkout under the default name
[[mentipy.json]] takes a nested repository with a root
[[mentipy.json]] of its own, a submodule holding another deck say, for
another copy, so a handout written into that submodule is not
recorded.  A target that is not recorded is therefore logged as a
warning, so that the author finds out from the build rather than from
a QR code that is never refreshed.

<<constants>>=
LOGGER = logging.getLogger(__name__)
<<functions>>=
def _spell_qr_path(
    target: Path, store_dir: Path, checkout: _Checkout
) -> str | None:
    if not target.is_relative_to(checkout.root):
        return target.as_posix()
    if _in_other_copy(target, checkout):
        return None
    return Path(os.path.relpath(target, store_dir)).as_posix()
@

A hand-edited store may hold something other than a string where a
path belongs, and one bad entry must not stop every later render.  We
read only the strings of a list and ignore everything else.

<<functions>>=
def _string_entries(value: Any) -> list[str]:
    if not isinstance(value, list):
        return []
    return [entry for entry in value if isinstance(entry, str)]
@

The store is written only when the question's list actually changed,
so rebuilding a deck does not rewrite the store once per question, or
when the store still holds legacy lists, so that the build migrates
them (below).

<<store methods>>=
def remember_qr_target(self, full_hash: str, qr_path: str | Path):
    """Remember that `full_hash` wrote a QR artifact to `qr_path`.

    A target in the store's checkout is recorded relative to the store's
    directory, so the store stays valid in every clone; a target in
    another copy of the project nested in the checkout (a worktree with
    its own copy of the store) is not recorded; any other target is
    recorded by its absolute path.
    """

    with self._lock:
        data = self._data()

        if full_hash not in data["questions"]:
            raise KeyError(full_hash)

        meta = data["questions"][full_hash]
        store_dir = self._store_dir()
        target = Path(qr_path).resolve()
        spelled = _spell_qr_path(target, store_dir, self._checkout())
        if spelled is None:
            LOGGER.warning(
                "not remembering QR code %s: it lies in another copy of "
                "the project, which carries its own %s",
                target.as_posix(),
                self.path.name,
            )
        stored = meta.get(QR_PATHS_KEY, [])
        paths = _string_entries(stored)
        if spelled is not None:
            paths.append(spelled)
        paths = list(dict.fromkeys(paths))
        if paths != stored:
            meta[QR_PATHS_KEY] = paths
        elif not _has_legacy_qr_targets(data):
            return
        self._write(data)
@

\paragraph{Legacy entries.}

An old [[qr_targets]] entry is trusted only when it is evidently this
checkout's own file: an absolute path on this platform, in the store's
checkout and not in another copy of the project, whose QR directory
exists.  Such an entry is respelled relative and moved to [[qr_paths]].
Every other legacy entry is dropped: another checkout's path, another
machine's, a non-string.  This is what finally prunes the personal
paths that older versions wrote into tracked stores.

The existing directory is evidence that separates this checkout's file
from a removed worktree under [[.claude/worktrees]], which has lost its
[[.git]] together with its directory and would otherwise pass for this
checkout's own.  The cost falls on a store migrated while its QR
directory was deleted, after a [[make clean]], say: those entries are
dropped, and the next build records them again.

The directory alone is not enough, though.  An older [[mentipy serve]]
recreated the directories of every remembered target, a removed
worktree's included, so a worktree's QR directory may exist again
without its [[.git]] or its store; and a worktree switched to a branch
without the store keeps its untracked QR directory too.  Such a path
still has the shape of another copy of the project: relative to the
checkout root it reads [[P/D/...]], where [[D]] is the store's own
directory relative to the root and [[P]] is not empty, as in
[[.claude/worktrees/x/modules/recap/slides/mentipy-obj/Q-ab12.pdf]] for
a store in [[modules/recap/slides]].  Legacy entries of that shape are
dropped as well.  A store at the root of its checkout has an empty
[[D]], so the shape says nothing there, and we accept that such a
resurrected directory can be migrated; the read below still keeps it
from being written once a worktree is created there again.

<<functions>>=
def _in_copy_layout(path: Path, checkout: _Checkout) -> bool:
    store_dir = checkout.store.parent.parts
    if not store_dir:
        return False
    parts = path.relative_to(checkout.root).parts
    size = len(store_dir)
    return any(
        parts[start:start + size] == store_dir
        for start in range(1, len(parts) - size)
    )


def _migrated_legacy_entry(
    entry: str, store_dir: Path, checkout: _Checkout
) -> str | None:
    path = Path(entry)
    if (
        path.is_relative_to(checkout.root)
        and not _in_other_copy(path, checkout)
        and not _in_copy_layout(path, checkout)
        and os.path.isdir(path.parent)
    ):
        return Path(os.path.relpath(path, store_dir)).as_posix()
    return None


def _has_legacy_qr_targets(data: dict[str, Any]) -> bool:
    return any(QR_TARGETS_KEY in meta for meta in data["questions"].values())
@

A legacy entry that is not absolute on this platform, a Windows path
read on Linux for instance, is a relative [[Path]] here and is never
relative to the root, so the first condition drops it as well.

\paragraph{Tidying on every write.}

The migration runs on every write of the store, for every question,
not only the remembered one.  A store keeps questions that no longer
appear on any slide, and no build remembers those, so a migration
driven by remembering alone would leave their old paths in the store
for ever.  The legacy key is removed once its entries are moved, so a
store pays for those probes only on its first write after the upgrade.

The same pass drops an absolute [[qr_paths]] entry whose QR directory
does not exist, which is the condition under which regeneration skips
it (\cref{sec:qr-regeneration}).  Without the drop, a printout
directory that has been removed would be warned about on every serve
for ever; with it, the next build that writes there records it again.
The price is churn in a store shared across machines: the first write
on a machine without the author's printout directory drops the entry,
and the author's next build adds it back.  A tracked store should not
carry such personal paths in the first place, which is the point of
the issue that motivated this section, so we accept it.  The pass also
drops whatever is not a string.  An entry is probed once per write,
and a store holds few absolute entries, so even a write per answer
during a lecture costs no more than a few [[stat]] calls.

<<functions>>=
def _directory_exists(entry: str) -> bool:
    path = Path(entry)
    return path.is_absolute() and os.path.isdir(path.parent)
<<store methods>>=
def _tidy_qr_targets(self, data: dict[str, Any]):
    store_dir = self._store_dir()
    checkout = None
    for meta in data["questions"].values():
        if QR_PATHS_KEY not in meta and QR_TARGETS_KEY not in meta:
            continue
        stored = meta.get(QR_PATHS_KEY)
        paths = [
            entry for entry in _string_entries(stored)
            if not _is_absolute_entry(entry) or _directory_exists(entry)
        ]
        if QR_TARGETS_KEY in meta:
            checkout = checkout or self._checkout()
            for entry in _string_entries(meta.pop(QR_TARGETS_KEY)):
                migrated = _migrated_legacy_entry(entry, store_dir, checkout)
                if migrated is not None:
                    paths.append(migrated)
        meta[QR_PATHS_KEY] = list(dict.fromkeys(paths))
@

\paragraph{Reading the targets back.}

Regeneration treats the entries differently (\cref{sec:qr-regeneration}),
so each remembered target comes back with its kind.

\begin{description}
\item[ [[own]] ] A relative [[qr_paths]] entry that resolves into this
  checkout and not into another copy, or a legacy entry that the
  migration would keep: this checkout's file.
\item[ [[outside]] ] An absolute [[qr_paths]] entry: a deliberate target
  outside the checkout.
\item[ [[foreign]] ] Anything else.  A legacy entry the migration would
  drop is reported so that regeneration can skip it visibly, before any
  write has migrated the store.  A relative entry can be foreign too,
  because the checkout it was recorded in need not be the checkout it
  is read in.  A deck built outside version control records a handout
  beside it as [[../handout/...]], the store's directory's parent
  standing in for the checkout; once the deck directory becomes a git
  repository of its own, that entry leads out of every clone.  And an
  entry migrated from a resurrected worktree directory of a store at
  the root of its checkout leads into another copy of the project once
  a worktree is created there again.  Checking where a relative entry
  lands, every time it is read, keeps both from being written.
\end{description}

A relative entry is joined onto the store's directory and normalised
lexically, like the [[os.path.relpath]] that wrote it, so [[../handout]]
comes back as the sibling directory itself.  Whether an entry is
absolute must not depend on the platform that reads it: a tracked store
can be written on Windows and read on Linux, where [[pathlib]] takes
[[C:/Users/alice/printouts/Q.png]] for a relative path that joined onto
the store's directory would make the server create [[C:/Users/alice]]
inside the deck; and on Windows a [[/home/...]] entry has no drive.  An
entry is therefore absolute when either platform's rules say so.  The
same file may be listed twice, under both keys or in two spellings
after a merge of two branches of a tracked store; it comes back once,
with the most trusted of its kinds.

<<constants>>=
OWN_QR_TARGET = "own"
OUTSIDE_QR_TARGET = "outside"
FOREIGN_QR_TARGET = "foreign"
<<classes>>=
class RememberedQrTarget(NamedTuple):
    """A remembered QR artifact: its path and how far to trust it.

    `kind` is `OWN_QR_TARGET` for a file of the store's checkout,
    `OUTSIDE_QR_TARGET` for a deliberate target outside it, and
    `FOREIGN_QR_TARGET` for an entry that does not belong to this
    checkout: a legacy entry of another checkout, or a relative entry
    that leads out of the checkout or into another copy of it.
    """

    path: Path
    kind: str
<<functions>>=
def _is_absolute_entry(entry: str) -> bool:
    return PurePosixPath(entry).is_absolute() or (
        PureWindowsPath(entry).is_absolute()
    )
<<store methods>>=
def remembered_qr_targets(self, full_hash: str) -> list[RememberedQrTarget]:
    """Return the remembered QR artifacts of `full_hash` with their kinds."""

    meta = self._data()["questions"][full_hash]
    store_dir = self._store_dir()
    checkout = self._checkout()
    found: list[tuple[Path, str]] = []
    for entry in _string_entries(meta.get(QR_PATHS_KEY)):
        if _is_absolute_entry(entry):
            found.append((Path(entry), OUTSIDE_QR_TARGET))
            continue
        path = Path(os.path.normpath(store_dir / entry))
        if path.is_relative_to(checkout.root) and not _in_other_copy(
            path, checkout
        ):
            found.append((path, OWN_QR_TARGET))
        else:
            found.append((path, FOREIGN_QR_TARGET))
    for entry in _string_entries(meta.get(QR_TARGETS_KEY)):
        migrated = _migrated_legacy_entry(entry, store_dir, checkout)
        if migrated is None:
            found.append((Path(entry), FOREIGN_QR_TARGET))
        else:
            found.append((store_dir / migrated, OWN_QR_TARGET))
    trust = (OWN_QR_TARGET, OUTSIDE_QR_TARGET, FOREIGN_QR_TARGET)
    kinds: dict[Path, str] = {}
    for path, kind in found:
        path = Path(os.path.normpath(path))
        kinds[path] = min(kinds.get(path, kind), kind, key=trust.index)
    return [RememberedQrTarget(path, kind) for path, kind in kinds.items()]
@


