\subsection{The locale the slides used}
\label{sec:recorded-locale}

A question's pages should be in the language of the slide that asked
it, and the slide's language is settled when the deck compiles: by the
helper's [[locale=]] keyword, or by [[mentipy.locale]] on the machine
that compiled it (\cref{sec:slide-labels}).  The server runs later,
perhaps on another machine whose configuration says something else, so
the LaTeX helpers record the locale each slide \emph{actually used}
next to the question, and the server reads it back
(\cref{sec:question-locale}).  Recording the effective locale rather
than the keyword is what makes a deck compiled on a Swedish-configured
laptop serve Swedish from an English-configured server.

Only a locale someone chose is recorded, though: the helper's keyword
or a configured [[mentipy.locale]], and only when Mentipy has a
catalogue for it, so that the slide really is in it.  A slide that is
English because nothing was chosen, or because the choice is a
language Mentipy lacks, records nothing.  That English is nobody's
decision, and recording it would be harmful: a trial render in a
terminal, a deck file that forgot the shared keyword or a co-teacher's
machine without the setting would break the language the store's
questions agree on (\cref{sec:agreed-locale}) and turn the index and
the waiting page English.  A later compile that chose nothing leaves an
earlier record in place, since [[register]] carries the key over.

The locale is presentation, like the QR targets: two decks in two
languages that ask the same question share its prefix, its QR code and
its answers, so the locale must never enter the hash.  It is therefore
an auxiliary key beside the question payload, which [[register]]'s
refresh carries over like any other key the question does not own.  A
question asked in two languages records the last one compiled.

<<constants>>=
LOCALE_KEY = "locale"
<<store methods>>=
def remember_locale(self, full_hash: str, locale: str):
    """Record that the slide of ``full_hash`` was in ``locale``."""

    with self._lock:
        data = self._data()

        if full_hash not in data["questions"]:
            raise KeyError(full_hash)

        meta = data["questions"][full_hash]
        if meta.get(LOCALE_KEY) == locale:
            return
        meta[LOCALE_KEY] = locale
        self._write(data)


def question_locale(self, full_hash: str) -> str | None:
    """Return the recorded slide locale of ``full_hash``, if any."""

    return _locale_record(self._data()["questions"][full_hash])
@

The two readers of the record go through one helper, which reports a
record only if it is text.  The store is a JSON file that people edit
by hand, and a number or a list where a locale belongs would otherwise
reach the server's language choice and fail every page that makes it,
the error pages included, with the connection closed and no answer.
Such a value is no record at all, like an absent one.

<<functions>>=
def _locale_record(meta: dict[str, Any]) -> str | None:
    value = meta.get(LOCALE_KEY)
    return value if isinstance(value, str) and value else None
@

Recording the locale is idempotent for the store, which is never
rewritten for an unchanged value, and the recorded value must outlive
the next registration of the same question, which rebuilds the
question's payload from scratch.  A recompile in another language
replaces it, and like the QR targets it stays out of the public view
of the question.

<<test functions>>=
def test_the_recorded_locale_survives_reregistration_and_follows_a_recompile(
    tmp_path,
):
    store = Store(tmp_path / "mentipy.json", min_prefix=4)
    question = OpenQuestion("Comment?")
    prefix = store.register(question)
    full_hash = store.resolve(prefix)
    assert store.question_locale(full_hash) is None

    store.remember_locale(full_hash, "sv")
    assert store.register(question) == prefix
    assert store.question_locale(full_hash) == "sv"

    store.remember_locale(full_hash, "en")
    assert store.question_locale(full_hash) == "en"
    assert "locale" not in store.metadata(full_hash)
    assert "locale" not in store.all_questions()[0]
    assert store.resolve(prefix) == question_hash(question)
@

The server also needs the recorded locales of the whole store: its
pages that belong to no question take the language the questions agree
on (\cref{sec:agreed-locale}).  The store reports each language once
and leaves the agreeing to the catalogue module, which knows the
languages.  Every question counts, orphans from an earlier build
included, since their pages are still served by their prefixes.  The
server asks on every request that needs the answer, so the reply
follows a recompile that records new questions while it runs, at the
price of one more read of the store, beside the several that most
requests make anyway.

<<store methods>>=
def recorded_locales(self) -> set[str]:
    """Return each locale that a question's slide recorded, once."""

    return {
        record
        for meta in self._data()["questions"].values()
        if (record := _locale_record(meta)) is not None
    }
@

A question without a record adds nothing, and a recompile in another
language shows at once.

